# RFC-0000: Rule Index and Conventions

Status: Active

## Abstract

Raytha engineering rules are numbered RFCs under `.cursor/rules/`. This
document lists them and defines the keyword convention they share.

## 1. Normative Keywords

The key words MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, and MAY in these RFCs are to
be interpreted as described in RFC 2119. MUST-level rules are enforced in
review and, where possible, by `tests/Raytha.Architecture.Tests`; deviating
from a SHOULD requires a stated reason in the PR.

## 2. Catalog

- RFC-0001 Architecture — Clean Architecture layers, Postgres-only persistence
- RFC-0002 CQRS — command/query/handler/validator structure via Mediator
- RFC-0003 Domain — entities, value objects, domain events
- RFC-0004 Data Access — `IRaythaDbContext` and EF Core patterns
- RFC-0005 Public Site — Razor controllers plus Fluid/Liquid templates
- RFC-0006 Admin SPA — React at `/raytha`, admin API, cookie auth
- RFC-0007 Testing — NUnit projects and architecture tests
- RFC-0008 Security — authorization policies, rate limits, ProblemDetails, headers
- RFC-0010 Admin List Query — list state lives in the URL
- RFC-0011 Webhooks — `[WebhookEvent]` commands and HMAC signing
- RFC-0012 Versioning and Migrations — `VERSION`, bump/check scripts, `v2_0_0`
- RFC-0013 Admin Editors — create is `/new`, edit is `/$id`, no editor dialogs

`pre-push-ci.mdc` is not an RFC; it points at the pre-push CI skill.

## 3. Scope

These rules describe Raytha 2.0 (`VERSION` 2.0.0 and later). Pre-2.0 code that
predates a rule is grandfathered where an RFC says so explicitly; new and
touched code follows the rule.

## 4. Amending a Rule

- Changing a MUST MUST come with the code and tests that make it true, in the
  same change.
- New RFCs take the next free number and MUST be added to the catalog above.
- `.cursor/standards.md` is the pre-2.0 long-form standards document. It is
  background reading; where it disagrees with an RFC, the RFC wins.
